1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4! { Welcome to my Chess Opening Course of the
Rapport-Jobava London System! The Rapport-Jobava London System is a infallible
opening which is moderately low and theory and quite easy to play. This
Opening system is very flexible. Bf4 Nc3 Targets are on the queenside. This
Opening System meets both aggressive and positional player's desire and
objective. Either tamed or bewildered the choice is up to you! } 3... Bf5 4. Nb5 { Nb5 might sound elementary but
either ways it actually part well! Why is that? What's the purpose behind the
move Nb5? Doesn't that sound elementary? SGM Hikaru Nakamura once threw a
tantrum and said this was garbage when Naroditsky successfully forked Hikaru
due to premove. Funny enough Nb5 is actually a very good idea in the
Rapport-Jobava London System. First it forces black to make this horrible move
Na6!? The only saving move thus a misplaced knight! This messes up black's
structure. It would take at least two tempos before black would be able to
bring his knight into a better square! Nb3 forces black to do the uncanny.
Keep that in your mind! } { [%csl Rb5,Rc7][%cal Rb5c7,Rf4c7,Yb8a6,Ya6c7] } (4. e3 { continuum: This is my recommendation. White immediately prepares f3-g4-h4: } 4... e6 5. f3 c5 6. g4 Bg6 7. h4 { For example } 7... h6 (7... h5 8. g5 Nfd7 9. Nb5) 8. Nb5) 4... Na6 { An uncanny and crampy move. Would take at least
two tempos before black could reroute his knight to a better place. Knight on
the rim Nh6!? A forced defensive move! } 5. a3 { a3! To avoid complications
since the position follows the same idea of the Rapport Jobava London System
with black having to step forward with Nb4! After c6! Pawn to a3 is pretty
much forced in here. } 5... c6 { The only good move is c6 in here. Everything else is
too passive. Pawn to c6 of course threatens to take the knight thus forcing it
to be evicted to the b5 square. } (5... c5 { c5 is another provacative move
which doesn't really sound very well because this further opens up the dark
squares around b8-f4. Position looks exploitable for white. Black needed to
counter white's dark square bishop before commiting to the move c5! } 6. dxc5 Ne4 7. b4 { White at ease was able to anchor the c5 pawn. White is up a piece
whereas black gets little compensation for the pawn sacrifice. } 7... g5 8. Be5 f6 9. Bc3) (5... e6 { e6 strengthens the center and black is planning to counter
white's dark square bishop with c6 thus kicking away the knight followed by
Bd6! } 6. e3 c6 7. Nc3 Bd6 8. Bxd6 { Other ideas includes the move Bg3, but I
prefer bishop takes bishop followed by f4! To strengthen white's control on
the e5 square. } 8... Qxd6 9. f4 O-O 10. Nf3 Nc7 11. Bd3) 6. Nc3 e6 { Now black gets
some sort of a Semi-Slav with the bishop on f5 and a knight on a rim a6 } 7. e3 Be7 8. Be2 O-O 9. g4 { More of a provacative move I have to admit. White lashes
out on the kingside with ideas of launching a pawn storm on the kingside. } 9... Be4 10. f3 Bg6 11. h4 h5 12. Nh3 b5 13. O-O *


