[StudyName "The Jobava London System (Rapport-Jobava System) - Complete Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Horwitz, Anti, Standard Defence #1 - 1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 (c5) Nf6 3. Bf4 e6"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HaFTAegs/Rmw2Nk2u"]

1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 { This particular chapter is dedicated to the"One" and "Only" main line
Horwitz Defence against the Jobava London. The Horwitz Defence can
basically be played against everything. By everything I mean "Everything". It can be played against the Queen's Gambit, Catalan Opening, English
Opening, London System, King's Indian Attack, and so on.Basically in here black's simple plan is to occupy the center, reinforce a hasty attack either on the center or the queenside depending on white's play. So on and so forth in the Jobava, the system remains very different. The idea
of Bf4 Nd5 is one of the key factor why the Horwitz's auto piloted move order wouldn't fit very well. Remember the idea of Bf4 Nd5! huh? Black either have to play a6 or Na6 just to stop this. Thus a tempo ahead and a complicatedly matter on the queenside makes this position quite unbearable! } 3... e6 { This moved has been played most of the time. The standard Horwitz Defence
against every system. Problem in here is that black allowed white to play
Nb5! Which is the reason why black cannot go auto pilot against the Jobava London unless the Nb5 threat has been covered. } (3... a6 4. e3 b5 { Black expands with a few ideas of attacking chances. In particular the
queenside. } (4... c5!? { Black challenges white's center. But in exchange white gets an extra
tempo whereas black would get little compensation on the prized center. } 5. dxc5 e6 (5... Qa5?! { Unnecessary since moving your queen on the rim would only waste a tempo.
Besides black has to make plans which piece will he use to recapture the c
pawn. With the queen?! Loses two tempos. } 6. Nf3 Qxc5 7. Qd3 { Flexible position, white is free to castle on the queenside which in this
case gives black attacking chances on the kingside. } 7... Nbd7 8. O-O-O) 6. Bd3! { Favorable position, flexible and stable development. White should make
plans when to castle. } 6... Bxc5 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. O-O b5 { White's objective is to open up the center with the move e4! } 9. e4 O-O 10. a3) (4... Bf5 5. Nf3 e6 6. Be2 Be7 7. Ne5 { The e5 square is often a good outpost. Keep that idea in mind would you? } 7... O-O 8. O-O Nc6 9. g4! { With attacking chances on tbe kingside but not necessarily what you
should do. You can leave the position before g4 and stick with your plans
and objectives. }) 5. Bd3 { Favorable position, flexible and stable development. White should make
plans when to castle. } 5... e6 6. Nce2 c5 7. c3 Nbd7 8. Nf3 Be7 9. O-O O-O 10. h3) (3... g6 { Some sort of a Semi-Grunfeld. White should just continue with his
standard set up. But aside from that everything is working well from here. } 4. e3 Bg7 5. Nb5 Na6 6. Nf3 c6 7. Nc3 Qb6 8. Rb1 Bg4 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O) 4. Nb5! { The threat of fork between the king and rook can only be stopped by Na6. This horribly looking move makes the knight dislodged from the game. It is misplaced, stuck on the rim and has to spend two tempos just to reinforce the knight into a better square. } { [%cal Rb8a6,Rb5c7] } 4... Na6 (4... Bd6?! { Another alternative which doesn't require the move Na6 to defend. This
may seem like a good option since this also strengthens black's center in
the future. But either ways you can't sleep too much on a sleep over! The problem behind this is that after knight takes bishop. White would have
the bishop pair. } 5. Nxd6+ cxd6 6. e3 Nc6 7. Nf3 O-O 8. h3 Re8 9. c3 e5 10. Bh2 { Despite of black's effort in breaking up the center. There's really not
much to be done in here for black since white's center is well fortified. }) (4... Bb4+ { Bb4+ is what @BossStephen usually plays against the Jobava London (Thanks for his recommendation!) pretty much } 5. c3 { l } 5... Ba5 6. e3 a6 (6... Nbd7 7. a4 a6 8. Na3 c5 9. Nf3 Bc7) 7. Na3 O-O (7... Bb6 8. Nf3 c5 9. Bd3 O-O 10. Ne5 Nc6 { This is the simple move, the alternate Bc7, sacrificing the c-pawn is also playable. } (10... Bc7 11. dxc5 Nbd7 12. Nxd7 Bxd7 13. Bg3 Rc8 14. b4!? { Moves like Nc2 or Bh4 are engine's top moves, suggesting to give up the extra pawn. } 14... b6 15. cxb6 Bxg3! 16. hxg3 Rxc3 17. Nb1! (17. Bxh7+?! { Equal according to engine, and looks cool! } 17... Nxh7 18. Qh5 f6 19. Qxh7+ Kf7 20. Nc2 { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bh7c2] } 20... Qc8 21. Nd4 e5 22. Ne2 Rc2 23. O-O Bf5 24. Qh5+ Bg6 25. Qf3 Be4 { Draw by repetition. }))) 8. Nf3 Nbd7 9. Be2 { Just continue with your normal development and you'll be fine. } 9... Ne4 (9... c5 10. dxc5 { Simply t } 10... Ne4) 10. Nb1 g5 { Brave berserk for black to ever try playing something like g5 then h5. But in exchange black weakens his king safety on the kingside. } 11. Bg3 h5 12. Bd3 h4 13. Nxh4 gxh4 14. Bxe4 dxe4 15. Qg4+! Kh7 16. Bxh4 f6 17. Qh5+) 5. e3 Be7 (5... c6 { Black sets up a Semi-Slav formation with the knight misplaced on a6!?
Would take several moves before black would be able to reinforce it in a
better place. } 6. Nc3 Qb6!? { Queen to b6 would often be met with the move Rb1! Everything would be
going in order. Would have been a very good position for white. } (6... Bd6 7. Bxd6!? { White is planning to set up a Semi-Stonewall Dutch formation against
black's wobbly position. } 7... Qxd6 8. Bxa6 bxa6 { Damages black's pawn structure plus we get a knight pair! } 9. Na4 Nd7 10. f4 Qb4+ 11. c3 Qb5 12. Nf3 O-O 13. b3 c5 14. Qd2 cxd4 15. cxd4 Bb7 16. Kf2 Nf6 17. Nc3 Qb4 18. Rhc1) 7. Rb1 c5 8. Nf3 Be7 9. g4! { With ideas of attacking chances on the kingside. We would win our pawn
shortly. } 9... Nxg4 (9... cxd4 10. exd4 Nb4 { Knight takes g pawn would be met with Rg1! Thus black's kingside would
crumble apart. } 11. a3 Nc6 12. g5 Nh5 13. Be3) 10. Rg1 h5 11. h3 Nh6 12. Rxg7) 6. Nf3 Nh5 { With obvious plan of simply removing the Jobava London bishop out of the
game. If that's the case then Bg3! } 7. Be5 f6 8. Bg3 Nxg3 9. hxg3 c5 10. c3 *


[Event "The Rapport Jobava System: Complete Repertoire: ____Chapter #1: \"ANTI-JOBAVA LONDON SYSTEM\" With Steinitz Countergambit____"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/AnndoYt3/xmCjae8w"]
[Date "2022.08.28"]
[White "Horwitz, Anti, Standard Defence #2"]
[Black "1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 (c5) Nf6 3. Bf4 e6"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/ShreksGonGiveItToYa"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "D01"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[StudyName "The Jobava London System (Rapport-Jobava System) - Complete Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Horwitz, Anti, Standard Defence #2 - 1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 (c5) Nf6 3. Bf4 e6"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HaFTAegs/sMKULrKk"]

1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c5 { In this particular chapter we would be talking about the "Steinitz Countergambit"This effective Anti-London is known to quickly equalize or even have a better position against the London System. That is because after c5 all what white can do is react and defend consequently. After c5 Black would proceed with Qb6! White has to defend the b pawn or otherwise lose a piece.Despite of this, today's modern theory had successfully repealed and made amends to the Anti-London "Steinitz Countergambit" where white sacrifice a pawn for piece activity.But does this "Stenitz Countergambit" be an effective Anti-London against the Rapport-Jobava? We'll see... } { [%cal Gc3b5] } 4. e3 { The Anti-London "Steinitz Countergambit" wouldn't ever be an effective as
it is against the London System. That is because the knight is on f3, with
move such as Rb1 the b pawn is finally protected. Now black's queen end up being misplaced on b6 without anything better to do. } 4... cxd4 { Let's take a look at this wing c pawn takes center d pawn. Black has
successfully take away one of white's center pawn against a wing pawn.
Leaving black with a strong initiative with the d and e center pawns.But not to worry! White's aim is piece development. White is ahead of
development and white's play would divert on the center. } (4... Bg4 { An energetic move which provokes white to either move the queen out or
f3! Two choices in here, but f3 is the most strongest amongst the purist
since this forces black's bishop to be evicted. } 5. f3 Bd7 (5... Bh5?! { Would be of no use since g4! Black would have wasted two tempos and
white's devastating blow on the kingside would provoke black's kingside. } 6. g4 Bg6 7. g5! { White has a very strong initiative and attack on the kingside. Black
would have a difficult time handling white's initiative on the kingside
since white's attack has been fueled by black's own tempos. } 7... Nh5 (7... cxd4 8. Nb5 Qa5+ 9. c3 dxc3 10. Nc7+) 8. Bxb8!! { Please, never EVER do this without any tactical justification. Bishop
takes knight forces black to take with the queen this losing the central d
pawn. Otherwise if rook takes bishop then Bb4+! Black is obligated to sacrifice a queen for it is the only option to continue the game. } 8... Qxb8 (8... Rxb8 9. Bb5+ Qd7 10. Bxd7+) 9. Bb5+ Kd8 10. Nxd5 { White Is already up a piece thus winning. Black's pieces are a
laughingstock. With no Incentive or initiative of the pawn sacrifice and
delayed development. Might as well as just resign in here. }) 6. Qd2! { Qd2! With clear intention of castling queenside. White's play would
divert on the kingside. With the help of the f3 pawn g4 h4 would come in
blitz maneuver. } 6... Nc6 7. O-O-O e6 8. g4! { "Attack Attack Attack Attack!" Push em baby yeah? Don't forget h4 soon.
Would have been a great idea to play later on. } 8... Be7 9. Nge2 O-O 10. h4) (4... e6?! { Pretty much straightforward. Black wants to avoid complication and
settled for e6?! to protect the c pawn. Why is this inaccurate you might
ask? Well Nb5 and black is forced to give in with Na6. A knight on a rim. Misplaced and horrible. Wouldn't want that would you? } 5. Nb5 Na6 { Na6 the only good move to stop the threat of knight on c2+ with a fork.
Na6 although force is an unfeasible and ugly move since the knight is on
the rim and it is misplaced! Would take two tempos just to straighten it up. } (5... Qa5+?? { b4! Black is forced to take with a check! Nc2 is inevitable in here.
Black's queen is forced to take the pawn. After queen takes pawn with a
check c3! Thus black cannot stop the idea of fork. The fork with the king and rook with the knight cannot be stopped. } 6. b4 { Black is forced to take the knight thus losing a tempo and the fork on c2
with the knight cannot be stopped } 6... Qxb4+ 7. c3 Qb2 8. Rb1 Qxa2 9. Nc7+) 6. c3 { Pyramid pawn structure as always seen in the London, Colle System or even
the Slav Defence. White has a powerful center in here. } 6... Bd7 7. a4 c4 { Since white's pawn is on a4. c4 was made possible. Threat of b3 wouldn't
be of any danger for black since he can simply take followed by Nc2 and
onwards. } 8. Na3 { Na3 just to shift away the knight from being taken by black's light
square bishop thus creating a double b pawn. Although the pressure on the
queenside would give white some attacking changes... I'm afraid the double b pawn creates more of a problem and risk since it would be hard to
defend or anchor it. } 8... Be7 9. Be2 O-O 10. Nf3 Nc7 11. O-O) (4... Nc6 5. Nb5 Qa5+ 6. c3 e5 7. Bxe5 Nxe5 8. dxe5 c4 9. exf6 Qxb5 10. Qd2 gxf6 11. Ne2) (4... Qb6 { Many "Anti-London" plays Qb6 against the London System. But in here it
doesn't work because it's the Jobava for Pete's sake! Rb1 is reinforcable
and Nb5! Is a grand idea in here. } 5. Nb5 Na6 6. dxc5 { Only good move is to take with the queen. Knight takes pawn is bad
because Nc2+! Losing the rook. } 6... Qxc5 7. Be5 { With clear intention of Be4 then Nxa7. } 7... Bd7 8. Bd4 Qc8 9. Nxa7 Qb8 10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Nb5 { White wins a piece! }) 5. exd4 Bg4 { An energetic move which provokes white to either move the queen out or
f3! Two choices in here, but f3 is the most strongest amongst the purist
since this forces black's bishop to be evicted. } 6. f3 (6. Be2 { Another safer approach would have been to play Be2, just to keep things
straight without risk I guess. } 6... Bxe2 7. Ngxe2 Nc6 8. Nc1!? { With idea of Ne3! To centralize the pawn onwards! } 8... e6 9. O-O Be7 10. N3e2 { Knight maneuver to keep in touch with the f4 square. } 10... O-O 11. Nd3 b5 12. c3 a5 13. Rc1 Qb6 14. Bg5) 6... Bd7 7. Bd3 { Nb5!? Isn't really useful since bishop takes knight, black would easily equalize and keep things balanced or under control.We play Bd3 to control the f5 square. Black's light square bishop would have been stuck down there under the gutter (Black's pyramid) } { [%csl Ye6,Yf7,Yd5,Yf5][%cal Yd3f5,Yf7d5,Ye7e6] } 7... Nc6 (7... Qb6! { As mentioned before Qb6 doesn't generally work because of Nb5 and the
"Reinforcable" Rb1. But in here with the light square bishop on e6 Nb5
isn't possible. To make amends to this simply Qd2 long castle 0-0-0 is a way to go! } 8. Nge2 a6 9. Qd2 e6 10. O-O-O Nc6 { With ideas of Nb4 and of course simply winning the d pawn. White has to
defend. } 11. Qe3 { Supports the d4 pawn. } 11... Nb4 12. a3 Nxd3+ 13. Rxd3 Qc6 14. g4 { Black has the queenside, White has the kingside. Interesting position
indeed! }) 8. Nge2 { Anchors the d4 pawn. Bg3 and Nf4 is quite a lovely idea in the near
future. } 8... Rc8 9. g4! { An energetic move in which white wants to shove a devastating attack on
black's kingside! What else could you ask for huh? } 9... e6 10. Qd2 Nb4 11. Nc1 { Nc1, in case of knight takes bishop we would successfully get our knight
centralized. Feasible and favorable! } 11... Nxd3+ 12. Nxd3 Be7 { Attack, attack, attack, attack! } { [%csl Yh4][%cal Yh2h4] } 13. h4 O-O 14. O-O-O { White does not have to worry about getting attacked by black. White's attack on the kingside would have been a devastating blow which provokes black to make amends and postpone the attack on the queenside. } { [%cal Yh1h8,Yd1g1,Ra7a5,Yh4h5,Rb7b5,Yg2g8,Yg4g5] } *


