1. d4 d5 (1... c5 2. d5 Nf6 3. Nc3 d6 4. e4 e6 { This move is where lots of variations begin } 5. Nf3 exd5 6. exd5 Be7 7. Be2 O-O 8. O-O Re8 9. Nd2 Bf8 10. Nc4) 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4! { Welcome to my Chess Opening Course of the Rapport-Jobava London System!
The Rapport-Jobava London System is a infallible opening which is
moderately low and theory and quite easy to play. This Opening system is very flexible. Bf4 Nc3 Targets are on the queenside. This Opening System
meets both aggressive and positional player's desire and objective. Either tamed or bewildered the choice is up to you! } 3... Bf5 { In this particular chapter we are going to talk about the one and only "Bf5" Symmetrical Jobava London."Black is following the same basic objective of the Rapport-Jobava. But in this scenario Black may have some ideas with putting off the light square bishop behind the pawn pyramid. e6-d5. But in here despite of black's logical idea Nb5! and Black would be forced to defend with Na6!? } { [%cal Re7e6,Rb7d5,Rc7c6,Rf7d5] } 4. Nb5 { Nb5 might sound elementary but either ways it actually part well! Why is
that? What's the purpose behind the move Nb5? Doesn't that sound
elementary? SGM Hikaru Nakamura once threw a tantrum and said this was garbagr when Naroditsky successfully forked Hikaru due to premove. Funny enough
Nb5 is actually a very good idea in the Rapport-Jobava London System. First it forces black to make this horrible move Na6!? The only saving move thus a misplaced knight! This messes up black's structure. It would take at least two tempos before black would be able to bring his knight into
a better square! Nb3 forces black to do the uncanny. Keep that in your mind! } 4... Na6 { An uncanny and crampy move. Would take at least two tempos before black
could reroute his knight to a better place. Knight on the rim Nh6!? A
forced defensive move! } 5. a3 { a3 needs to be Played because after c6 Nc3 Nb5! That would have been a
horrible smack down don't you think? Black is the one who's on offense. } 5... c6 (5... c5 { White's goal is to weaken and take over one of white's central pawns.
Well since everything is forced Nb5! Black is once again forced to defend
with Na6! } 6. dxc5 Ne4 7. b4 { Despite all the odds the pawn has successfully been anchored. White would
have been up a piece and winning in here. } 7... g5 8. Be5 f6 9. Bc3) (5... e6 { defensive move amongst positional players. Pawn to c6 and we are back to
the Slav Defence. } 6. e3 c6 7. Nc3 Qb6 { Rb1 is the best move in here. } 8. Rb1 Be7 9. Be2 O-O 10. Nf3) 6. Nc3 e6 7. e3 { Both opposing sides fixates their pawn structure... } { [%cal Rf2d4,Rb7d5,Rf7d5] } 7... Be7 8. Be2 O-O 9. g4! { White is now launching a devastating attack on the kingside! This would
have been a horrible and uncanny situation for black who's gonna be fixated
in defensive position! } 9... Be4 { Be4 is black's most finest defence in here. But hold on can't black just
drop back to g6 maybe? Well... We will get to that in a minute or so. } (9... Bg6 { Bg6 is also possible. But this forces black to make this weakening move h6!? Which is very uncanny since pawn to g5 would have forced black to take away one of his key defensive piece. Without the pawn to f3 I'm afraid the defensive h5 maneuver was made impossible. } { [%csl Rf3,Rh5][%cal Bf2f3,Rh5d1] } 10. h4 h6 11. g5 hxg5 12. hxg5 Nh7 13. Bd3) 10. f3 Bg6 11. h4 { h5 black has a strong fortified kingside. Although opening up the h file
for black might seem as a weakening move. It's for the best just so to
extinguish part of white's attack. } 11... h5 12. Nh3 b5 13. O-O { No fear of death! To triumph without risk is to triumph without glory! } *


[Event "The New London System - London Buckingham System: Anti-Slav Defence #1 - 1. d4 c6 Bf4 f3 (e5, Bf4)"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/bUhH2SrO/2pCymKn3"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[White "Anti-Slav Defence #1"]
[Black "1. d4 c6 Bf4 f3 (e5, Bf4)"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Shreksify"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Opening "Blackmar-Diemer Gambit Declined: O'Kelly Defense"]
[StudyName "The Jobava London System (Rapport-Jobava System) - Complete Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Anti-Slav Defence #1 - 1. d4 c6 Bf4 f3 (e5, Bf4)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HaFTAegs/FzG4eIUl"]

1. d4 c6 { This is the Slav Defence. The Slav Defence is known for it's dynamic and
positional play. Despite it's reputation black's counterplay heavily relies
on breakthroughs such as on the "Center" or "Queenside." In here the main line is usually c4 followed by a "Catalan" structure. But instead of c4
which is heavily theoretical. We would go for Nc3! Our Rapport-Jobava structure! } 2. Nc3 d5 3. f3!? { e4 is straight up possible in here but that would eventually transpose
into the "Classical Caro Kann". In the Classical Caro Kann white would get
a slight edge but position would have been opened and would give black a quite flexible position. Instead of transposing into the Classical Caro
Kann let's go for f3!? In the move pawn to f3 at least white would be able to recapture with the f pawn thus having a powerful center. A semi opened f file, and of course white gets a easy and flexible play with chances of attacking black's kingside. } 3... Nf6 (3... e6 4. e4 { With the knight and the f pawn supporting the e pawn thrust. White would
dominate on the center with an absolute advantage! } 4... Nf6 5. Nh3! { With ideas of placing the knight to f2 to protect the e4 thrust. White
has to do whatever it takes to keep the center fortified. } 5... Be7 (5... c5! { Black tries to mess up white's well structured center. Of course white
needs to defend this by Be3! } 6. Be3 Nc6 7. Bb5 { Pins the knight thus loosen up black's control on the center. } 7... cxd4 8. Qxd4 Bd7 9. Qd2 Bb4 10. Bxc6 Bxc6 11. e5 Nd7 12. Bd4 O-O 13. O-O-O) 6. Be2 O-O 7. Nf2 Nbd7 8. O-O dxe4 { Pawn takes e4 would help white since the f pawn would eventually be
opened. } 9. Ncxe4 c5 10. c3 { Everything is holding up pretty well against black's less fortified
center. } 10... cxd4 11. Qxd4 Nxe4 12. Qxe4 Nf6 13. Qc2 Qc7 14. Bg5 Ng4 15. Nxg4 Bxg5 16. Rad1) 4. e4! { With the knight and the f pawn supporting the e pawn thrust. White would
dominate on the center with an absolute advantage! } 4... dxe4 { White cannot take with the f pawn since pawn takes pawn would lead into a
complicated position with e5! For black. White wouldn't be able to savour
the center and an open f file for long unless white makes amendments with knight takes e pawn! } 5. Nxe4 e5 6. Nxf6+ Qxf6 7. Be3 Bd6 8. Qd2 { White can shift his counterplay both on the kingside and center. White
can now safely castle on the queenside. } 8... O-O 9. O-O-O { Chapter #10 - Slav Defence - 1. d4 c6 2. Bf4 d5 } *


