1. e4 { Apologies if I started with the move e4. This was taken from my study
"Unorthodox e4 Collection" And I have an extensive repertoire on playing
against the Caro Kann. The Exchange Variation is one of them. However the theory pretty much remains the same after 1. d4 c6 2. e4! d5 } (1. d4 c6 2. e4 { After d4 and then c6 white could play e4 and everything would transpose
into the old fashioned Caro Kann Defence. Just don't confuse this with e4
at first move. Play d4, if black plays c6 then play e4! }) 1... c6 2. d4 { e4! Effectively transposing into Caro Kann. Wait hold on why e4? Isn't
this the Caro Kann and not the London System? What does this have to do
with the London System? Wait hold up, before you have any doubts on this particular variation. We are going to settle for an Exchange Caro Kann
followed by the improvised London System via Bd3 and c3! } 2... d5 { The Caro Kann Defence The Caro Kann Defence is the most solid opening
choice against the move e4. The Caro Kann Defence starts with the move e4
c6 d4 d5! The Caro Kann is favoured amongst Chess legends such as Smyslov, Karpov, Botvinnik, Tal, and even Magnus Carlsen! } 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 { The Exchanged Caro Kann is the simplest and easiest way to handle the Caro Kann Defence. White is settling for a Rubinstein structure or the Improvised London System. White is planning to play c3 Bf4 Nf3 Nd2 0-0 and is headed for a very nice game! } { [%csl Rf5][%cal Rd3f5] } 4... Nc6 5. c3 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rb2d4] } 5... Nf6 (5... Qc7 { Perhaps a bit provacative, first of all black wants to control the f4
square which dislodges white from making progress with the dark square
bishop. Black is heavily invested upon the e5! Breakthrough aswell. } 6. Na3! { With intention of course playing this Nc2 idea which strengthen white's
d4 pawn. Useful for black's threat of e5! } (6. h3! { Another idea is to play this particular move h3! Which by then prevent
black from playing Bg4. By then white can play Be3 Nd2 Nf3! } 6... e5 7. dxe5 Nxe5 8. Bc2 { Slides the light square bishop away from the danger. White can now play
Nd2 and of couse Nf3 followed by short castle 0-0. } 8... Nf6 9. Nd2 Bd6 10. Ngf3 Ng6 11. O-O O-O 12. Re1) (6. g3 { Perhaps a bit unorthodox but quite a reasonable idea. White wants to play
Bf4 and remove the queen from controlling the f4 square. Black has to play
e5 to try and give some challenges to white but may backfire since black is under-developed, } 6... e5 { [%csl Ye5] } 7. dxe5 Nxe5 8. Bf4 Nxd3+ 9. Qxd3 Qb6 10. Nf3 Qxb2 11. O-O Qxa1 { Despite of white's loss on the rook. Black's queen is pretty much stuck in here and soon we would eventually be able to win it back. } { [%csl Ra1,Rb1,Rb5,Re8][%cal Rd3b5,Rb5e8,Rb8b1] } 12. Qb5+ Bd7 13. Qxb7 Rc8 14. Ne5 Be6 15. Nxf7 Bxf7 16. Qxc8+ { Effectively winning a piece! }) 6... a6 { Of course black doesn't want white to play Nb5! So he plays a6 for safe
measures. } 7. Nc2 Nf6 8. h3 { Prevents the standard idea of Bg4! } 8... e6 9. Qe2 Bd6 10. Nf3 O-O 11. O-O Re8 12. Bg5 { Trying to dislodge black's control on the e4 square. } 12... Nh5 13. Rfe1 Nf4 14. Bxf4 Bxf4 15. a4 e5 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. Nxe5 Qxe5 18. Qxe5 Rxe5 19. Rxe5 Bxe5 20. Re1 { With a slight advantage for white due to his piece activity. }) 6. Bf4 { We are going to develop our bishops first before knight. Although this might contradict the opening principal of developing knight before bishop. We are controlling the important f4 square and d6 square! Black has four major response in here which would all be covered in this particular course. Bg4, e6, g6, Qb6. } { [%csl Rd6,Gg6,Ge6,Gg4,Gb6][%cal Rf4d6,Rc1f4,Gc8g4,Gd8b6,Ge7e6,Gg7g6] } 6... Bg4 { Black is settling for a French Defence like position with the c pawn
missing. It would be difficult for black to find counterplay in this
particular position due to the fact that the queenside is the only minority outbreak in which would give black counterplay. } (6... g6 7. Nf3 Bg7 8. O-O O-O 9. Re1 { The line recommended by GothamChess's Caro Kann Course. Black wants to
voluntarily open the g file thus weakening his kingside if white takes the
light square bishop. As usual white may have fallen for an illusion in which white is actually the one penetrating black in here since black's
kingside is weakened. But Kh8 Rg8 followed by a stack up of rooks leaves white's kingside vulnerable. } 9... Bf5! 10. Bxf5 (10. Bb5 { We wouldn't wanna exchange bishops in here. That would effectively give
black the e4 square and black has a strong attack using the g file for his
rook to take advantage of. Instead we can play something like Bb5! Or even Be2! } 10... Qb6 11. Qa4) (10. Be2)) 7. Qb3 Na5 8. Qa4+ Nc6 (8... Bd7 { Quite necessary for black to bring back his bishop to d7 to attack the
queen and parry white's check on black's king. } 9. Qc2 Qb6 10. Nf3 e6 11. Nbd2 Bb5 12. Ne5 Be7 13. O-O Bxd3 14. Nxd3 O-O 15. Rfe1 Rac8 16. Nf3 Ne4 17. h4 Nc4 18. Re2 Bd6 19. Rae1 Bxf4 20. Nxf4 Qd6 21. g3 h6 22. Kg2 b5 23. a3 a5 24. Nd3 Rc7 25. Nfe5 Rfc8 26. f3 Nf6 27. g4) 9. Nd2 e6 10. Ngf3 Bd6 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. O-O { Chapter #11 - Slav Defence - 1. d4 c6 2. Bf4 d5 } *


