1. d4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Bf4 { In here we are going to take a look at another alternative in which can
be applied against the Slav Defence. This time we are going to stick with
our Jobava London set up. I'm actually very hesitant to share something like this since this would effectively give you part of my opening prep. But
for the love of God I'm willing to share this regardless of my disadvantage. I wouldn't wanna hinder any important information! } 3... Nf6 4. e3 e6 5. g4! { Once black plays something like e6 pawn to g4! Should be accounted here. Grob idea! Basmanic indeed! Point in here is white wanna play something like g5! Followed by h4! } { [%csl Gg4,Gg5,Gh4,Gh3,Gf3,Ge5][%cal Gg2g4,Gg4g5,Gh2h4,Gg1h3,Gg1f3,Gf3e5] } 5... Qa5 { Black has two alternatives in here. Either he plays something like Qa5
trying to create havoc on the queenside or Bb4. Both of which doesn't
really help dislodge white's attack. } (5... Bb4 6. f3 O-O (6... Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 Qa5 8. Kf2! { Bongcloud theory! Point in here is as soon as black takes the c3 pawn
black pretty much commits his queen on the queenside leaving the kingside
compromised. } 8... Qxc3 (8... c5 9. h4 Nc6 10. g5 Nh5 11. Bd6 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gf3f4] } 11... b6 12. f4 g6 13. Nf3) 9. g5 Nfd7 10. Bd6 Qa5 11. h4 Qd8 12. Qc1 Nb6 13. Qa3 Na6 14. Rb1 Nc7 15. h5 Nb5 16. Rxb5 cxb5 17. h6 g6 (17... Qxg5 18. f4 Qg6 19. Bd3 f5 20. Be2 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Ge2h5] } 20... Bd7 21. Bh5) 18. Be5 Qxg5 19. Bxh8) 7. h4 Re8 8. h5 { I don't care regardless of what other people would say. I want you to
shove the h pawn on black's kingside. Bust it open! Black would probably
play something like h6 to avoid such ideas. } 8... Nfd7 (8... h6!? { Opens up thousands of attacking chances. I wouldn't wanna be black in
situations like this. It would suck to be black in here I have to admit. I
wouldn't wanna be stuck defending! } 9. Qd2 Nbd7 10. O-O-O b5 11. g5! { Bust open black's kingside! This is just horrendous for black. } 11... e5 12. dxe5 hxg5 13. h6! { White doesn't care about that bishop. As long as we bust open the
position we would be winning! } (13. Bxg5?! { I mean, goal in here is to bust open black's kingside no matter the cost.
If white simply took the pawn then this would give black the opportunity to
loosen up white's furious attack. } 13... Nxe5 14. h6 Ne4) 13... gxf4 { White can move his queen to g2 or h2 to give a devastating blow on black's kingside. But regardless of black's state with accurate play black can try to hold on a bit longer. } { [%csl Gh2][%cal Gd2h2,Gd2g2] } 14. hxg7 Kxg7 15. exf4 Rg8 16. Nge2 Kf8 17. Nd4 Bc5 18. Nce2 Ne8 19. Rh6 { Attacking the c6 pawn. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gh6c6,Gd4c6] } 19... Nb8 20. Bh3 { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gf1h3] } 20... Bxh3 21. Rxh3 Nc7 22. Rh7 Ne6 23. Nxe6+ fxe6 24. Nc3 { White pretty much has all the fun in here. Black's king safety is compromised. } { [%csl Gh1,Gf5][%cal Gd1h1,Gd2d3,Gf4f5] }) 9. Qd2 e5 10. dxe5 Nxe5 11. h6 g6 12. Bxe5 Rxe5 13. Qd4) 6. a3 Ne4 { The problem with Ne4 is that white would play f3 soon which would then
fuel white's wrath on the kingside. Keep in mind that if black doesn't
really do anything serious then simply play something like f3 then h4! } 7. Ne2 Be7 8. f3 Nd6 (8... Bh4+ 9. Bg3 Nxg3 10. hxg3 Be7 11. f4 Qb6 12. Rb1 O-O 13. g5 { [%csl Gd3,Gh5][%cal Ge2c1,Gc1d3,Gd1h5] }) 9. Ng3 O-O 10. e4! { White here wants to secure his advantage by playing e4! Controls the f5 square. Black cannot longer play f5 to loosen up tension. Other than that, this effectively forcing black to either exchange pawns which would then open hundreds of opportunities for white to unleash his fury or simply hold this off and call this a day. } { [%csl Gf5,Ge4,Gg4][%cal Ge4f5,Gg4f5] } 10... b5 { In situations like this you wouldn't really wanna play e5 since this
would open up an opportunity for black to breakout with f6! } (10... dxe4 11. fxe4 f6 12. Qd2 c5 13. dxc5 Qxc5 14. O-O-O) 11. Qd2 Nd7 12. g5 Nc4 13. Bxc4 bxc4 14. O-O Qd8 15. Kh1 Bb7 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 16. e5 { White now controls the f6 square which makes f6 not possible. We can play e5 in here since black doesn't have enough manpower to create possible counterplay on the queenside. Everything is pretty much shut down. One thing we know for sure is white has a good attacking chance on the kingside. } { [%csl Ge5,Gf6][%cal Ge4e5,Ge5f6,Gg5f6] } *


[Event "The Rapport Jobava System: Complete Repertoire: ____Chapter #4 \"KID/PIRC DEFENCE\" Tamed or Agressive Move Order____"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/AnndoYt3/zeAqNvqQ"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[White "King's Indian, Pirc, Grunfeld #1"]
[Black "1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/ShreksGonGiveItToYa"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B07"]
[Opening "Pirc Defense"]
[StudyName "The Jobava London System (Rapport-Jobava System) - Complete Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "King's Indian, Pirc, Grunfeld #1 - 1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HaFTAegs/CUipVcqR"]

1. d4 Nf6 { Nf6 black wants to divert the game into a King's Indian or a
Semi-Grunfeld structure. But in here after the move Nc3! Black would have
to play d4 thus transposing back to the position we studied with the Horwitz Defence. Well other than that if black refuses and play g6!?
Then simply e4! The move pawn to e4 was made possible because of the knight on c3. Black would have to play d5 or d6!? We'll get into that. } 2. Nc3 { White wants to play e4! Black has to play d5 or otherwise if let's say
for example g6!? Aiming for a Grunfeld or King's Indian structure. Then
simply e4! Then white is roughly doing better than black with his central advantage. } 2... g6!? { The move g6!? Allows the move e4! Giving white spacious and central advantage. } { [%csl Ge4,Gf4][%cal Ge2e4,Gf2f4] } 3. e4 { Simply taking over the center. Was made possible because of the knight to
c3 move order. } 3... d6 { There are two particular variations in which I recommend in here. The
Austrian Attack or the "English" or "Yugoslavia" Attack structure.
Everything heavily depends on your decision. } 4. Be3! { Be3! Is a very suitable and feasible system against the King's Indian or
pirc with the Jobava London System. White is settling for the "English"
or"Yugoslavia" structure where white castles queenside. Plays f3 followed by g4! and h4! A continuous attack on the devastated kingside! } (4. f4 { f4! The Austrian Attack. The Austrian attack is suitable for positional
players who want to gain spacious advantage. White's attack would either
divert on the center or kingside (Via Bird/Dutch Kingside Attack). } 4... Bg7 5. Nf3 { Bishop to e2 followed by short castle 0-0 should be enough and feasible
for white's particular position. I do believe that white has the advantage
in here because of the spacious advantage. } 5... O-O 6. Bd3 { Two main moves, either Nc6 or even c5!? Both of which are included in
here.For starters knight to c6 prevents black from making progress on the
central breakthrough since the c5 pawn is blockaded. On the other hand c5 tempts white to exchange pieces and simplify. } 6... Nbd7 { Let's just say black doesn't really do anything aside from going forward
with his usual King's Indian or Pirc structure and idea. That is the
breakthroughs with c5 or Re8 e5! Well simply e5! White gains a favorable position with a spacious advantage. } (6... Nc6!? { White should play e4! With the help of the Nc6!? Move order black would
have quite a problem with pawn breakthroughs in here. } 7. e5 dxe5 8. fxe5 Nd7 9. Ne2 Nb4 10. Be4 f5 11. Bd3 Nxd3+ 12. Qxd3 c5 13. Qb3+ Kh8 14. c3) (6... c5 { Quick tip in the Austrian Attack: It would perhaps be better if white
were to give up the center if black's main objective is to throw as much
pieces as possible in breaking up the center. I've been through many positions such as the Advance Caro Kann, French, and even Pirc. I've gotten
the spacious advantage but the problem is black's main objective shifted on the center. I've tried to hold unto it until it became my untimely downfall.So general rule would have been to '"Give up the center if necessary". } 7. dxc5 dxc5 8. e5 { Wherever the knight goes. White's idea is Qe2 Bd2 0-0-0 followed by g4! Attacking black's center. } { [%cal Rd1e2,Rc1d2,Re1c1] } 8... Ng4 9. h3 Nh6 10. Be3 Qa5 11. g4 Rd8 12. Qe2 Nc6 13. O-O-O) 7. e5 Ne8 { Be3 0-0 is one heck of a good idea in here. Simply develop and stick with your opening principals and fundamentals. You can't just heavily anchor your pawn journey with openings. Cannot be fixated like that. You can't sleep too much on a sleep over! } { [%cal Rc1e3,Re1g1] } 8. Be3 c5 9. O-O { As a general advice "Give up the center of necessary" White has to give
up the center or otherwise have some dents and iniquities on the center.
I'm the Pirc or King's Indian black heavily relies on the pawn breakthroughs with c5, e5, or even f5! You cannot just defend and defend and defend!
You wouldn't like that!Rather than being busy defending and giving out tenacious fortification which is roughly exhausting. Simply give up the center an d you'll be fine. } 9... cxd4 10. Bxd4 dxe5 11. fxe5 Nxe5 12. Bxe5 Bxe5 13. Nxe5 { Qd4 with a check and fork is the main idea in here. But nevertheless the
f file is open and white has a very good position in here. } 13... Qd4+ 14. Kh1 Qxe5 15. Qd2 Nf6 { [%csl Re1][%cal Ra1e1] } 16. Rae1 { White is up in development and roughly dominating black's position.
Black's forces is outmatched against white's active and we'll placed
forces. }) 4... Bg7 5. Qd2 Nbd7 { Simply castle queenside and you would most likely be fine. Black would
aim for the queenside whereas white would aim for the kingside. A double
edged sword indeed! } 6. O-O-O Ng4 { One of the most common idea amongst positional players. Chop off the
pieces who are currently active in participation on the objectified goal. } (6... b6!? { We would take a look at what would happen if black were to ignore
everything else on the plate and decided to go for a quiet but simple
objective with Re8 and e5! } 7. f3 { Simply f3 followed by g4! b4! and h4! Quick reminder that you should
first develop your pieces and organize them in according divisions but with
same objective of winning. The others will defend whereas the others would attack! } 7... Bb7 8. Kb1 O-O 9. Nge2 { Could be reinforced to c1 in case black shoves a pawn storm on the
queenside. } 9... Rc8 10. g4! { "To Be Continued" }) 7. Bf4 { We can't exchange our precious and noble dark square bishop! That's our
key piece in attacking black's well fortified kingside! } 7... e5 { Time to give up the center! } 8. dxe5 dxe5 9. Bg3 { White dominates on the d file. } 9... Bh6 10. f4 O-O 11. Be2 Ngf6 (11... f5 { A logical continuation but heavily weakens the light squares around black's position. } { [%cal Ya2g8] } 12. exf5 gxf5 13. Nf3 c6 14. Ng5) 12. h4 { The Attack continues! } 12... Qe7 13. h5 exf4 14. Bxf4 Bxf4 15. Qxf4 c6 16. Rf1 { I would prefer white in here. Although the opening sequences and move
order is quite dubious. White's idea of attacking kingside has never been
so difficult indeed! } *


