[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HaFTAegs/qwrJgof3"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 { [diagram] } (2. Nc3 { Nc3 was originally the move order in here. From my previous Jobava London
System course Nc3 is much preferable since it is flexible. However it can
be applied in the Accelerated London System aswell. White could have played something like d4 Nf6 e3 and then there goes b6! After b6 white should play
Nc3! } 2... e6 (2... d6 { [#] } 3. Nf3 (3. e4 e5) 3... Nbd7 { Black may try to play d5-the Black Lion } 4. Bf4 { We would be going for our normal London System } 4... c6 5. e3 Qb6 6. Rb1 g6 7. Bd3 Bg7 8. O-O O-O) (2... g6 { [diagram] } 3. e4 { This is another interestine. We will treat the King's Indian Defence as
the Sicilian Dragon. } 3... d6 4. Be3 Bg7 5. Nf3 (5. Qd2? { This move loses the bishop pair } 5... Ng4) (5. f3 { This is the Yugoslav or the English Attack. We would be launching a
devastating attack against black. } 5... O-O 6. Qd2 Nc6 7. O-O-O e5 8. d5 Ne7 9. g4 { [%cal Rh2h4,Rh4h5,Rh5h6] } 9... Ne8 (9... Kh8 { [%eval 32766,0] Example game } 10. h4 h5 11. Nge2 hxg4 12. h5 gxh5 13. Ng3 Ng6 14. Nxh5 Nxh5 15. Qh2 Nh4 16. Qxh4 Qxh4 17. Rxh4 gxf3 18. Rxh5+ Kg8 19. Bd3 f6 20. Rg1 Bd7 21. Bh6 Rf7 22. Nd1 Kf8 23. Rxg7 Rxg7 { 1-0 (23) Sargissyan,S (2283)-Warkentin,J (1878) Lille 2014 }) 10. Bd3 { [%eval 32764,0] Example Game } 10... c6 11. dxc6 bxc6 12. Bc4 Be6 13. Qd3 Qa5 { 1-0 (13) Ponkratov,P (2530) -Losev,D (2381) Zvenigorod 2008 }) 5... O-O 6. Qd2 Ng4) (2... d5 { The only way the Grunfeld is possibly employable in here is if white goes
with the Horwitz move order with d5 before jumping to the conclusion with
Bg7?! Followed by d5?! } 3. Bf4 g6 4. Nb5! { The threat of fork between the king and rook can only be stopped by Na6. This horribly looking move makes the knight dislodged from the game. It is misplaced, stuck on the rim and has to spend two tempos just to reinforce the knight into a better square. } { [%cal Rb8a6,Rb5c7] } 4... Na6 5. Nf3 Nh5 6. Bd2 Bg7 7. e3 c6 8. Nc3 Nc7 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O) 3. Bf4 b6 { A solid opening, the Queen's Indian is a reputable option for players who
seek a "risk-free" game. Players of all levels can play the Queen's Indian,
although it is advisable to have a basic understanding of positional concepts before venturing into this opening. } 4. e4 { Of course white needs to take over the center but if you don't like this.
Well continue with your standard Jobava-London System set up and you'll
most likely be fine. } 4... Bb7 5. Bd3 Bb4 (5... c5 6. Nge2 { Protect the center at all might! Black would take the d pawn of course
but either ways white would recapture with the knight. } 6... cxd4 7. Nxd4 d6 (7... Bc5 8. Ndb5 d6 9. O-O O-O 10. Na4 Bb4 11. c3 Bc5 12. Nxc5 dxc5 13. Nc7) 8. Bg5 Nbd7 9. Qe2 Be7 10. O-O-O) 6. f3 { White anchors the center, this is much easier to play and safer I have to
admit. White doesn't really have to fear much with black's assault on the
center. } 6... d5 7. Ne2 O-O 8. O-O) 2... g6 { [diagram] [#] This chapter is dedicated to the King's Indian Defence, the
Czech Defence, and of course the Grunfeld. } 3. Nc3 { Generally speaking, black would often play d5 since e4 is coming. In that case we are back into the grunfeld line. But if black is faithful to the king's indian defence then play this. } { [%cal Rc3e4,Re2e4] } (3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Be2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. h3 Re8 8. c4 { equality after White plays c4 instead of Nd2 (0.0) } 8... e5 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Qxd8 Rxd8 12. Bxe5 { Trap losing a pawn after Black mistimes e5 move (+1.2) }) (3. Nf3 { This is a normal London Buckingham System line. } 3... d5 (3... Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O { Generally, if I meet the King's Indian with the accelerated London, I
prefer to control the center than to play the Buckingham since controlling
the center is much mor efficient and easier. }) 4. g3 { This is another London buckingham System line } 4... Bg7 5. Bg2 O-O 6. O-O) 3... Bg7 { Playing the obvious 3... Bg7 instead of d5 allows White to play the very
strong move e4, Rosen also offers a tactic for attacking even if Black
defends with 3... d5 and then castles too early instead of blocking the advance of the h-pawn } (3... d5 4. e3 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gh2h5] } (4. g3 Bg7 5. Bg2 O-O 6. Nf3 { We are back in the grunfeld line, I actually prefer to play the
Buckingham in here since this is no longer the King's Indian but rather the
Grunfeld } 6... c5 7. e3 Nc6 (7... Qb6 { Just wanna show you what happens after Qb6 since in the grunfeld line our
knight is often on d2 not on c3. } 8. Qc1 cxd4 (8... Nc6 { [%csl Rb6,Rc6,Rd4][%cal Rb6d4,Rc6d4] } 9. O-O cxd4 10. exd4 Nxd4 11. Nxd4 Qxd4 12. Nb5 Qb6 13. Nc7 Rb8 14. Nxd5 { We take back our pawn. }) 9. exd4 Nc6 10. O-O { Generally we are fine in here }) 8. O-O) 4... Bg7 5. h4 O-O (5... h5 6. Be2 O-O 7. Nf3 c5 { (-0.1) } 8. dxc5 Qa5 9. O-O Qxc5 10. Na4 Qa5 11. c4 Bf5 12. Nc3 dxc4 13. Bxc4 Nc6) 6. h5 Nxh5 7. Rxh5 gxh5 8. Qxh5 { (+2.2) }) 4. e4 { IM Eric Rosen recommends this position for White to play with a variation of the London System against the King's Indian Defense, opening up a strong kingside attack where the Black king is expected to be castled soon } { [%csl Gf2][%cal Gf4h6,Gh2h4,Gf2f3,Gd1d2,Ge1c1] } 4... c5 (4... d5 5. e5 Ne4 6. Nxe4 { We can simply capture the knight } 6... dxe4 7. Bc4 c5 8. c3 O-O 9. Ne2 Nc6 10. O-O { You are already doing good in here. Just make sure to guard the d4 pawn. } 10... Qb6 11. Qc2 { We are fine in here } { [%cal Rc2e4] }) (4... d6 { The King's Indian on the board } 5. Qd2 { This is the most safest line to play } (5. h4 O-O 6. h5 (6. d5 Nbd7 7. h5 gxh5 8. Rxh5 Nxh5 9. Qxh5) 6... Nxh5 7. Rxh5 gxh5 8. Qxh5 { Personally, this is one of my favorite line } 8... Bxd4 { White sacrifices a pawn and rook for a knight but eventually ruin's the
kingside structure of black } 9. Nf3! Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 f6 11. Bc4+ e6 12. O-O-O Qe8 13. Qh3 Nc6 14. Rh1 Rf7 15. Qh5 Bd7 16. Bh6 Qe7) 5... O-O { [diagram] } (5... c6 { [#] This is the Czech Defence } 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O-O Nbd7 8. Be2 { We get a desirable position! } 8... b5 9. e5 b4 (9... dxe5 10. dxe5 Ng4 11. Ne4 f5 12. Neg5 Nxf2 13. e6) 10. Na4 Ne4 11. Qe3 Qa5 12. b3) 6. O-O-O c5 (6... Nbd7 { Black has two moves now, c5 or e5 } 7. Nf3 { Prevents e5! } 7... c5 8. Bh6 Qc7 9. h4 { We launch an attack right away! }) (6... Bg4? 7. f3 { Bg4 only helps us }) 7. d5 Nbd7 8. Nf3 a6 (8... Rb8 { Black wants to expand on the queenside! } 9. e5 dxe5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Bxe5 Ne4 12. Qf4 Nxc3 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. Qe5+ Kg8 15. Qxc3 { Comfortable position for us! }) (8... a5 { What if black tries to attack us on queenside? Well play Bb5 } 9. Bb5) (8... b6 9. Bb5 a6 (9... Bb7 10. Bh6 a6 11. Bc6 Bxc6 12. dxc6 Nb8 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. e5 { Another variation }) 10. Bc6 { b6 gives us the opportunity to play this }) 9. h3 b5 10. Re1) 5. d5 O-O 6. Nf3 d6 7. a4 Nh5 8. Be3 Bg4 9. Be2 Nd7 10. O-O Bxf3 11. Bxf3 Bxc3 12. bxc3 Nhf6 13. Qe2 Re8 14. a5 Ne5 15. c4 { Chapter #9 - King's Indian, Pirc, Grunfeld - 1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6 3. e3 d6 } *


