1. d4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 (2... d5 { The only way the Grunfeld is possibly employable in here is if white goes
with the Horwitz move order with d5 before jumping to the conclusion with
Bg7?! Followed by d5?! } 3. Bf4 g6 4. Nb5! { The threat of fork between the king and rook can only be stopped by Na6. This horribly looking move makes the knight dislodged from the game. It is misplaced, stuck on the rim and has to spend two tempos just to reinforce the knight into a better square. } { [%cal Rb8a6,Rb5c7] } 4... Na6 5. Nf3 Nh5 6. Bd2 Bg7 7. e3 c6 8. Nc3 Nc7 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O) 3. e4 d5?! { This particular chapter is dedicated to none other than the Grunfeld
Defence. The Grunfeld Defence stadts with Nf6 g6 and then d4! But in here
the Grunfeld doesn't work once white occupies the center with Nc3 and e4! If black refuses to make amends with e6 and follows up with d5?! Then as
you can probably guess e5! White dominates on the center and black has a misplaced knight. } 4. e5 Ne4 (4... Ng8 { Black waste a tempo moving his knight twice in the opening. Nothing much
to cover in here aside from developing onwards. } 5. h3 e6 6. Nf3 c5 { Of course black would respond violently with c5. But either ways black is
under developed and the kingside is at large stake. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. Bd3 Nc6 9. O-O Nge7 10. Bg5 O-O 11. Bf6) (4... Nh5! { Some sort of a wierd and unorthodox Norweigian variation of the King's
Indian except the pawn is fixated in d5. } 5. Be3 c6 6. h3 Qb6 7. b3 Ng7 8. Nf3 f6 9. Bd3 fxe5 10. Nxe5 Nd7 11. f4) 5. Nxe4 dxe4 6. c3 Bg7 (6... c5 7. d5 Bg7 8. Qa4+ Bd7 9. Qxe4) 7. Bb5+ c6 8. Ba4 O-O 9. Bc2 c5 10. Bxe4 *


