1. d4 e6 (1... c5 { This is the Old Benoni Defence. The old Benoni Defence's main objective
is of course to try and disrupt white's center thus the main objective of
white is to either respond with passive maneuvers or extend the pawn to the fifth-rank. } 2. d5 { Eventually there's no other better move other than pushing the pawn
forward thus gaining a spacious advantage. But black would constantly
pressurize white's extended pawns. Should be wary of black's assault. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 d6 (3... e6 { Black would constantly pressurize white's extended pawns. Should be wary
of black's assault. White should of course defend with e4! } 4. e4 d6 5. Bc4 { White should hold on to his spacious advantage until it is uneasy and
impossible to maintain. } 5... exd5 6. Nxd5 { Nxd4?? Qe2! Followed by f3! Thus losing a piece for black. } 6... Nbd7 7. Nf3 Be7 8. Nxf6+ { Exchanging Knights would loosen black's assault on the center. } 8... Nxf6 9. Qe2 O-O 10. O-O) 4. e4 g6 { Black sets up a King's Indian Structure. This is going to feel uneasy
since black's counterplay would heavily rely both on the kingside and
queenside. } 5. Be2 Bg7 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O e6 (7... a6 { If black doesn't really do anything aside from getting some sort of
counterplay on the queenside with a6 b5! White would attack black's weakest
link. The kingside! } 8. a4 Bg4 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Bxf3 Qa5 11. g4 { White's attention would be to attack white's kingside. Black would aim
for the queenside whereas white's would be the kingside. }) 8. Bf4 exd5 9. exd5 { White's e pawn would be a bit of a burden to defend. Which is why white
is going to place the rooks on e and d file and of course to provoke
weaknesses. } 9... Na6 10. Nb5 Ne8 11. c3) 2. Nc3 c5 { This is the new Benoni system. The usual Franco-Sicilian or
Speilmann-Benoni. Black's standard objective is of course to counter
white's central expansion. But aside from that to have a some sort of a Sicilian like position. Simplest solution is to exchange pawns
and go easy alongside with our life. If black refuses and play e5 Simplest solution is to exchange pawns and go easy alongside with our life. If black refuses and play e5 then e4! We are back to the usual Old Benoni. } 3. d5 { Advancing the pawn is the best move in here since this gives white a bit
of a spacious advantage. But since our knight is on c3 we cannot really
establish a well fortified spacious advantage. } 3... exd5 4. Nxd5 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ { Black's pawn structure is a wee bit difficult to manage. Backward d pawn,
remains a heavy target for white. } 5... Qxf6 6. e4 { Everything seems to simplified very well. We get some sort of a "Drunk"
Sicilian Defence. } 6... Nc6 7. Ne2 Be7 8. Nc3 O-O 9. Bd3 Nb4 10. O-O *


