forward thus gaining a spacious advantage. But black would constantly
pressurize white's extended pawns. Should be wary of black's assault. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 d6 (3... e6 { Black would constantly pressurize white's extended pawns. Should be wary
of black's assault. White should of course defend with e4! } 4. e4 d6 5. Bc4 { White should hold on to his spacious advantage until it is uneasy and
impossible to maintain. } 5... exd5 6. Nxd5 { Nxd4?? Qe2! Followed by f3! Thus losing a piece for black. } 6... Nbd7 7. Nf3 Be7 8. Nxf6+ { Exchanging Knights would loosen black's assault on the center. } 8... Nxf6 9. Qe2 O-O 10. O-O) 4. e4 g6 { Black sets up a King's Indian Structure. This is going to feel uneasy
since black's counterplay would heavily rely both on the kingside and
queenside. } 5. Be2 Bg7 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O e6 (7... a6 { If black doesn't really do anything aside from getting some sort of
counterplay on the queenside with a6 b5! White would attack black's weakest
link. The kingside! } 8. a4 Bg4 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Bxf3 Qa5 11. g4 { White's attention would be to attack white's kingside. Black would aim
for the queenside whereas white's would be the kingside. }) 8. Bf4 exd5 9. exd5 { White's e pawn would be a bit of a burden to defend. Which is why white
is going to place the rooks on e and d file and of course to provoke
weaknesses. } 9... Na6 10. Nb5 Ne8 11. c3 { Chapter #13 - Benoni Defence - 1. d4 e6 (1... c5) 2. Bf4 c5 } *


