1. d4 f5 { The Dutch Defence is a chess opening characterised by the moves: 1. d4 f5
Black's 1...f5 stakes a claim to the e4-square and envisions an attack in
the middlegame on White's kingside; however, it also weakens Black's kingside to an extent. } 2. f3!? { My idea against the Dutch Defence is quite a bit unorthodox but playable.
I've played this myself with success. The move pawn to f3!? fights for the
e4 square. White can also open up the position with e4! But this would become complicated and to sum it all up, it offers a lot of risk. I suggest
developing all of your pieces first before attacking the center with e4. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 d5 { [%csl Rd5,Re4,Ye5,Rf5][%cal Yd4e5,Rf5e4,Rd5e4] } (3... e6 4. Be3! { White is planning to tuck in the dark square bishop to f2 to cover up the
dark squares around the kingside. Adds up in aid against the Dutch's
dangerous attack! } 4... Be7 5. Qd2 O-O 6. O-O-O { White was able to castle in ease in the queenside. White can now play the
move Bf2 and of course the breakthrough e4! } 6... Nc6 7. Bf2 d6 { White has to play Kb1 first in order to avoid complications on the
center. Just to keep things safe for short. } (7... d5 { Stonewall Dutch The Stonewall Dutch is a setup where Black creates pawn
"teeth" in the center on the light squares f5, e6, d5, and c6. This gives
Black a great amount of space and control of e4, at the expense of a certain weakness of the dark-squares. Pros: Black does not lack space. Control
of e4 might lead to a kingside attack. } 8. e3 a6 9. Nge2 b5 10. Kb1 { It would be nice if white was able to centralize his knight via Nf4 and
Nd3! Just to increase the pressure on the e5 square. } 10... Bd7 { [%cal Re2f4,Rf4d3] } 11. Nf4 Rb8 12. Nd3 { [%csl Re5,Yc6][%cal Yc6e5,Rd3e5,Rd4e5] } 12... Bd6 13. Ne2 e5 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. Nxe5 Bxe5 16. Nf4 c6 17. h4 { Black does have some good attacking chances on the queenside. White has
to shift his attention to the kingside by launching a devastating attack
starting with shoving the h pawn! }) 8. Kb1 a5 9. e4 fxe4 10. Nxe4 Nxe4 { White has a strong central pawn but either ways black would throw a heavy
assault on white's wobbly center. } 11. fxe4 { [%csl Re4,Rd4] } 11... Bg5 12. Qe1 e5 13. Bc4+ Kh8 14. d5 { White now dominates on the center. White can then shift his attention to
the kingside by launching a devastating attack starting with shoving the h
pawn! } 14... Nd4 15. h4) (3... g6 { Anyone playing the Dutch Defence on a regular basis will undoubtedly
suffer the occasional disaster. It is a tough opening to handle and one
needs to be on good form to harness the particular type of energy it creates. Yet it is an excellent way to unbalance the situation and to play
for a win against 1 d4. People who like to draw games need to look elsewhere. } 4. e4 fxe4 (4... e6 5. Bg5 Be7 6. Qd2 Nc6 7. O-O-O) 5. fxe4 d5 6. e5 Ne4 7. Nxe4 dxe4 8. Bc4 Bg7 9. Be3 Rf8 10. Ne2 c5 11. c3 Nc6 12. Qb3) 4. Be3! { White is planning to tuck in the dark square bishop to f2 to cover up the
dark squares around the kingside. Adds up in aid against the Dutch's
dangerous attack! } 4... e6 5. Bf2 { White has certain ideas with Nh3 Nf4 and Nd3 to centralize the knight and
of course to increase the pressure on the e5 square. } 5... Be7 { [%cal Rg1h3,Rh3f4,Rf4d3] } 6. Nh3 O-O 7. Nf4 c5 (7... c6 8. e3 Qc7 9. Qd2 c5 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. O-O-O) 8. e3 Nc6 9. Be2 Qb6 10. Rb1 cxd4 11. exd4 Bd7 12. O-O { Chapter #17 - Dutch Defence - 1. d4 f5 2. Bf4 (2. Nc3) (2. f3) } *


