1. d4 { [diagram] } 1... e6 { This kind of opening could lead into certain transpositions. One is the
Queen's Indian, one is the Horwitz and many other variations. } (1... b6 { [#] This is the English Defence. Usually tranposes to the Owen Defence
after the move e4. } 2. e4 { The Owen Defence is considerably one of the most solid and dynamic
opening. The Owen Defence may look ridiculous at first but no. You see
Black has the opportunity to strike back towards white's expansion towards the center. The fight towards the center is fierce and intense. White
may have a hard time to tackle black and keep the advanrage. Thereby Im giving you a opening line in which should provide you enough resources to battle against the Owen Defence } 2... Bb7 { [diagram] } (2... c5 { Black's center strike is considerably bad since white can safely advance
the pawn forward to d5 giving white a comfortable play. } 3. d5 d6 (3... e6 { Black may immediately strike towards our expansion of our center at the
cost of an extra tempo. } 4. Nf3 a6 (4... exd5? 5. Qxd5! { [%csl Ra8][%cal Rd5a8] } 5... Nc6 6. Ne5 Qf6 7. Bc4 { We already have a superior position in here, } { [%csl Rc4,Yc6,Yc8,Rd7,Re5,Ye8,Yf6,Rf7][%cal Rd5d7,Rd5f7] }) 5. a4 d6 6. dxe6 fxe6 7. Ng5 { [%eval 186,8] The weaknesses around white's kingside really tells everything you need to know about this line. } { [%cal Rh5e8] }) 4. Bb5+ Bd7 (4... Nd7 { Black's another alternative in which does not prevent white from
exploting black's weaknesses around the position. } 5. Bc6 Rb8 6. Nc3 Bb7 7. Bxb7 Rxb7 8. a4 g6 9. f4 Bg7 10. Nf3 { [%eval 199,11] } 10... Ngf6 11. O-O O-O) 5. a4 Bxb5 6. axb5 { We lock down the weaknesses around black's position } { [%csl Rb7,Yb8,Rc6][%cal Ra1a8,Rb5c6,Rd5c6] } 6... g6 7. f4 Bg7 8. Nf3 Nf6 9. Qe2 O-O 10. c4 Nbd7 11. O-O) 3. Bd3 { [%csl Rg2][%cal Rb7g2] } (3. Nc3 { Nc3 is another alternative, but most probably the mov c3 was better since
it sustains the d4 pawn all through out the middlegame. } 3... e6 4. Nf3 d6 5. a4 a6 6. Bd3 Nd7 7. O-O h6 8. Re1 Ngf6 9. h3 Be7 10. Bf4 { This is the kind of set up we are usually aiming for with the move
Nc3.0-1 (51) Nepomniachtchi,I (2784) -Radjabov,T (2765) chess24.com INT
2020 }) 3... e6 (3... f5 { This is a gambit, black exploits the fact that the g pawn is unprotecterd
and if white accepts the pawn capture. Black would be winning the rook.
Problem with this position for black is that he is risking his king's safety for the sake of an illusion. Theres a clear refutation along in thi line. } 4. exf5 Bxg2 5. Qh5+ g6 6. fxg6 Bg7 7. gxh7+ Kf8 8. Ne2 Nf6 9. Qh4 Bxh1 10. Bg5 { You see, black would have to sacrifice two pawns and a knight in order to
bring his king to safety. AThis is just a huge gamble for black especially
the fact that his king is at the edge of the cliff. } 10... d5 11. Nf4 Qd6 12. Ng6+ Ke8 13. Nxh8 Qe6+ 14. Kf1 Bxh8 15. Nd2 Be4 16. Nxe4 dxe4 17. Re1 Nc6 18. Bxf6 Bxf6 19. Qh5+ Qf7 20. h8=Q+ Bxh8 21. Qxh8+ { Black's position was hopeless. Certain sacrifices was be made just to
keep black's king from execution! } 21... Kd7 22. Qxa8 exd3 23. cxd3 Qf3 24. Qg8 Qxd3+ 25. Kg1 Nxd4 26. Qg4+ Ke8 27. Kg2 Nc2 28. Rd1 Qh7 29. Rd5 Kf7 30. Rf5+ Ke8 31. Qa4+ Kd8 32. Rf8# { 1-0 (32) Slibar,M (2166)-Borisek,A (1915) Ljubljana 2021 }) 4. Nf3 c5 (4... d6 { Thus, this line couuld also transpose into a Hippo System. A solid wating
move for black. } 5. Nbd2 Nd7 6. Nf1 Ngf6 7. Ng3 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Re1 e5 10. c3 { This is the line that needs to be addressed. We would be using this set
up against black's Hippo System. } 10... Re8 11. h3 Bf8 12. Qc2 g6 13. Be3 Qe7 14. Rad1 Bg7 { 1-0 (39) Giri,A (2777)-Radjabov,T (2763) chess24.com INT 2021 }) 5. c3 Nf6 6. Nbd2 Be7 7. O-O O-O { [#] This is the kind of set up we are going to aim for almost all the
time. }) (1... Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. Bf4 (3. c4 b6 4. a3 Bb7 5. Nc3 d5 (5... Be7 6. d5 d6 7. e4 exd5 8. cxd5 O-O 9. Be2 c6 10. O-O { [%eval 148,10] }) 6. e3 Be7 7. Qc2 O-O 8. Be2 Nbd7 9. O-O) 3... b6 4. e3 Bb7 5. Be2 Nh5 6. Bg3 Nxg3 7. hxg3 Be7 8. Nc3 O-O) 2. e4 { [diagram] } (2. Nc3 { If you do not want to go for a transposition of the French Defence, then
this is what you need to do. } 2... d5 3. Bf4 Nf6 4. g3 Be7 5. Bg2 O-O 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O { We are back from our London Buckingham System. }) 2... b6 { [#] } (2... f5 { Bad Opening line for black, heres how we will punish this. } 3. exf5 exf5 4. Qh5+) (2... Nf6 3. e5 Nd5 4. c4 { Stockfish 14.1: } 4... Ne7 5. Nf3 d6 6. exd6 cxd6 7. Nc3 { This line isn't considerably bad, but either ways this is how we deal
with it. } 7... d5 { [%eval 126,8] }) (2... d5 { Transposes to the French Defence, I'll give you a bit of an idea what you
should be playing against the French Defence. } 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Nc6 7. Nf3 Nge7 8. Be2 O-O (8... Qa5 9. Bd2 O-O 10. O-O) 9. O-O) 3. Nf3 Bb7 4. Bd3 Nf6 5. Nbd2 (5. e5 { Another alternative choice for white } 5... Ne4 6. O-O f5 7. c4 Be7 8. Re1 O-O 9. Nbd2 Nxd2 10. Bxd2 c5 { [%eval 137,9] }) 5... c5 6. c3 Be7 7. e5 Nd5 { Stockfish 14.1: } 8. Nc4 O-O 9. O-O f5 10. exf6 Nxf6 11. Re1 Nc6 { Chapter #7: - English-QID-Systems, London Buckingham System: Complete
Repertoire Guide: London Buckingham System: Queen's Indian Defence,
https://lichess.org/study/uT7Q2OHt/CyPq17LP, 2022.01.08 } *


