1. d4 e5 { As part of my extensive London Buckingham course. I think that this
particular gambit needs to be covered. I've faced this so many times and I
have to admit I didn't really know the refutation into this and I didn't seem to care (Too lazy to study it.) As a result I've lost many games. I
didn't want those things to happen aswell to you. So as I result I included the refutation to the Englund Gambit aswell! The Englund Gambit is a well recognized trap in which black sacrifices a pawn after the move d4 then e5! Black would be aggrevated and would try his best to prove his
initiative. } 2. dxe5 Bc5 { Black's intended move sequence of d6 and Ne7 makes for a comical aside. A
common snare that International Master Eric Rosen and other chess material
creators have discussed at length. } (2... d6 3. exd6 Bxd6 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. e4 Nc6 7. Bd3 Ne5 8. O-O Bg4 9. Bg5) 3. Nf3 d6 4. exd6 { What, the pawn beat the knight? Bishop captures the f2 pawn! This means
the queen has been dethroned. } 4... Ne7 { [%cal Rc5f2,Rd8d1,Rd6e7] } 5. e3 { Covered the threat and the idea. } 5... Bxd6 6. Bc4 O-O 7. O-O { Englund Gambit - Jobava London System } *


