1. d4 e5 { As part of my extensive London Buckingham course. I think that this
particular gambit needs to be covered. I've faced this so many times and I
have to admit I didn't really know the refutation into this and I didn't seem to care (Too lazy to study it.) As a result I've lost many games. I
didn't want those things to happen aswell to you. So as I result I included the refutation to the Englund Gambit aswell! The Englund Gambit is a well recognized trap in which black sacrifices a pawn after the move d4 then e5! Black would be aggrevated and would try his best to prove his
initiative. As part of my extensive Jobava course. I think that this particular gambit needs to be covered. I've faced this so many times and I have to admit I didn't really know the refutation into this and I didn't seem to care (Too lazy to study it.) As a result I've lost many games. I didn't want those things to happen aswell to you. So as I result I included the refutation to the Englund Gambit aswell! The Englund Gambit is a well recognized trap in which black sacrifices a pawn after the move d4 then e5! Black would be aggrevated and would try his best to prove his
initiative. } 2. dxe5 d6 (2... Bc5 { A funny sideline where black is planning to play d6 and Ne7! A well known
trap covered by IM Eric Rosen and other various Chess content makers. } 3. Nf3 d6 4. exd6 { Pawn takes knight?? Bishop takes f2 pawn! Thus the queen is gone! } 4... Ne7 { [%cal Rc5f2,Rd8d1,Rd6e7] } 5. e3 { Covered the threat and the idea. } 5... Bxd6 6. Bc4 O-O 7. O-O { [%cal Rb1c3,Rc1d2,Rd1e2,Ra1d1] }) (2... f6 { Another alternativr in which deals with opening up the f file. In here
white should simply just develop and he'll be fine. } 3. exf6 Nxf6 4. Nf3 Bc5 5. Bf4 O-O 6. e3 Nc6 7. Bd3 d6 8. Nbd2 Be6 9. O-O) 3. exd6 Bxd6 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. e4 Nc6 7. Bd3 Ne5 8. O-O Bg4 9. Bg5 *


