1. d4 e5 2. dxe5 Nc6 (2... d6 3. exd6 Bxd6 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. e4 Nc6 7. Bd3 Ne5 8. O-O Bg4 9. Bg5) 3. Nf3 Qe7 4. Bf4 Qb4+ 5. Bd2 Qxb2 6. Nc3! { This is the key move to refute black's dubious opening. } 6... Bb4 { [%cal Rb4c3] } (6... Nb4 { [%csl Rc2][%cal Rb4c2] } 7. Nd4 { [%cal Gd4c2] } 7... c5 { Don't worry about the knight, the move Rb1! Would save the day! } { [%csl Rd4] } 8. Rb1 { [%csl Rb2] } 8... Qa3 9. Ndb5 { [%csl Ra3] } 9... Qa5 10. a3 { [%csl Rb4] } 10... Na6 (10... Nc6 11. Ne4 (11. Nd5 { [%csl Ra5][%cal Rd2a5,Rb5c7] })) 11. Ne4 Qd8 12. Nbd6+ Bxd6 13. Nxd6+ Kf8 14. e4) 7. Rb1 { [%csl Rb2] } 7... Qa3 (7... Bxc3 8. Rxb2 Bxb2 9. c3 Ba3 10. Qb3 Be7 11. e4) (7... Qxc3!? { Quite a strong gambit, despite of black's tactical sacrifice for the
queen in exchange for two minor pieces its still quite hard to play against
this. Black doesn't really have a problem on the opening. } 8. Bxc3 Bxc3+ 9. Nd2 Bxe5 10. e4 (10. e3 $18) 10... Nge7 11. Bb5 O-O 12. O-O) 8. Nd5 { It's the Queen's wanderings that make the pawn on c7 so vulnerable. } { [%csl Rb4,Rc7][%cal Rd5c7] } 8... Bxd2+ (8... Ba5 9. Rb5! { Trying to eliminate one of black's only defender against the c7 threat of fork. } { [%csl Rc7][%cal Rd2a5] } 9... Bb6 10. Rxb6 axb6 11. Nxc7+ Kd8 12. Nxa8 Qxa8 13. Qb1) 9. Qxd2 { [%csl Rc7][%cal Rd5c7] } 9... Kd8 (9... Qxa2 { Trying to put the fire out. } { [%csl Rb1] } 10. Rd1 { [%csl Rc7][%cal Rd5c7] } 10... Kd8 11. Ng5 { In a manner similar to the variants seen in the Morra Gambit, white also assaults from the other side! } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rg5f7] } 11... Nh6 12. e6! { Black's position being braded on the center should be one of black's
takeaway target in here. We have to bust open the positin then! } 12... d6 { An attempt to keep things closed for the time being. } (12... fxe6 13. Nxe6+! dxe6 14. Nc3+ $18 { This nasty discovered check decides. } { [%csl Ra2,Rd8][%cal Rc3a2,Rd2d8] }) 13. e7+ { A move that is a little bit odd? The main idea is that an early Nxc7 is
doomed to failure because to the threat posed by Kxc7 Qxd6+, Kb6! in a
position in which the black King is unexpectedly not in danger. After the move e7+ Nxe7, it is now impossible for Nxc7 to save the game since Kxc7
Qxd6 is checkmate. } 13... Kd7 (13... Nxe7? 14. Nxc7! { [%csl Rd6,Ra8][%cal Rd2d6,Rc7a8] } 14... Kxc7 15. Qxd6#) 14. Nxh7!! { One more really great move! White makes the threat Nf8+, and if the rook enters the game, the square e8 will be weakened, allowing for a forced mate to occur. } { [%cal Rh7f8] } 14... Rxh7 15. e8=Q+ Kxe8 16. Nxc7+ Kf8 (16... Ke7 17. Qxd6#) (16... Kd7 17. Qxd6#) 17. Qxd6+ Kg8 (17... Ne7 18. Qd8#) 18. Qd8+! { You can't escape the wrath of "GRU!" } { [%csl Rg8,Rh7][%cal Gg8h7] } 18... Nxd8 19. Rxd8#) 10. Qg5+ Nge7 11. Nxe7 Qxe7 12. Qxg7 Rf8 13. Ng5 f5 14. exf6 Qxf6 15. Qxf6+ Rxf6 16. Nxh7 *


