[FEN "rn1qkb1r/ppp1pppp/5n2/3p1b2/3P1B2/2N5/PPP1PPPP/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 4 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ I'm guessing that you already know what to do in here. }
4. Nb5! { Nb5 might sound elementary but either ways it actually part well! Why is
that? What's the purpose behind the move Nb5? Doesn't that sound
elementary? SGM Hikaru Nakamura once threw a tantrum and said this was garbagr when Naroditsky successfully forked Hikaru due to premove. Funny enough
Nb5 is actually a very good idea in the Rapport-Jobava London System. First it forces black to make this horrible move Na6!? The only saving move thus a misplaced knight! This messes up black's structure. It would take at least two tempos before black would be able to bring his knight into
a better square! Nb3 forces black to do the uncanny. Keep that in your mind! } 4... Na6 5. e3 c6 6. Nc3 { Don't worry about losing a tempo! Black's knight is literally on the rim!
Would take a lot of time to organize his knight! } *


