1. d4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. f3!? { e4 is straight up possible in here but that would eventually transpose
into the "Classical Caro Kann". In the Classical Caro Kann white would get
a slight edge but position would have been opened and would give black a quite flexible position. Instead of transposing into the Classical Caro
Kann let's go for f3!? In the move pawn to f3 at least white would be able to recapture with the f pawn thus having a powerful center. A semi opened f file, and of course white gets a easy and flexible play with chances of attacking black's kingside. } 3... e6 4. e4 { With the knight and the f pawn supporting the e pawn thrust. White would
dominate on the center with an absolute advantage! } 4... Nf6 5. Nh3! { With ideas of placing the knight to f2 to protect the e4 thrust. White
has to do whatever it takes to keep the center fortified. } 5... c5! 6. Be3 Nc6 7. Bb5 { Pins the knight thus loosen up black's control on the center. } 7... cxd4 8. Qxd4 Bd7 9. Qd2 Bb4 10. Bxc6 Bxc6 11. e5 Nd7 12. Bd4 O-O 13. O-O-O *


