1. d4 { http://www.viewchess.com/cbreader/2014/6/26/Game127724859.html } 1... Nf6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Bf4 { This London/Veresov hybrid will probably gain increased attention, as not
only Jobava but other elite GMs have tried it the past several years,
including Mamedyarov himself a couple of times, and especially Hikaru Nakamura (at least seven times!). } 3... Bf5 4. f3 e6 5. g4 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. e3 c5 { Rare, but it had hitherto been successful in the earlier, non-GM trials. } (7... a6 { was tried by Ian Nepomniachtchi against Nakamura in round 9; that game
eventually finished in a draw. "Nepo" also played it against Jobava in
round 13 of the same event, but lost that game. }) 8. h5 { Oddly, this obvious move was a novelty. } 8... Bh7 9. Nb5 Na6 10. c3 Be7 11. Bd3 Bxd3 12. Qxd3 Nd7 { Aiming to prevent g4-g5 so he can safely castle. } 13. Ne2 (13. Nd6+?! Bxd6 14. Bxd6 Qb6 { is not what White had in mind. }) 13... O-O 14. a4 Bf6 15. Bd6 Re8 16. f4 Nb6? { Way too optimistic. Mamedyarov's king is inadequately guarded, but in his
defense it took some brilliant play by Jobava to prove it! } (16... Bh4+ { is playable, but White isn't really harmed by the loss of castling
privileges, and the position remains complicated after Kf1 or Kd1. }) 17. g5! hxg5 18. h6! g6 19. fxg5 Bxg5 20. h7+ { Not allowing the pawn to be blockaded on a light square. In that case it
would be very difficult to get at Black's king. } 20... Kg7 21. Be5+! f6 (21... Bf6 22. Nd6 Bxe5 23. dxe5 Qe7 (23... Qg5 24. Nf4! $16) 24. O-O-O { is also extremely dangerous for Black. White has more than enough for the
sacrificed pawn, and will not be in any hurry to swap the knight on d6 for
the rook. }) 22. Nd6 { White had better, though this is still strong - especially if Black
doesn't give up his queen. } (22. Bf4! $16) (22. Nf4 $16) 22... Rh8? (22... fxe5 { was essentially forced. } 23. Nxe8+ Qxe8 24. h8=Q+ Qxh8 25. Rxh8 Rxh8 26. O-O-O $14) 23. Nf4! Bxf4 24. Bxf4 { A normal and good move, and the sequel shows that Jobava knew what he was
doing. There was a flashy and faster win available though. } (24. Rg1! g5 25. exf4 fxe5 26. Rxg5+ Qxg5 (26... Kf8 27. Rg8+! Rxg8 28. hxg8=Q+ Kxg8 29. Qg6+ { and mate next move. }) 27. fxg5 Rxh7 28. O-O-O $18) 24... g5 25. O-O-O! Nc4 { It might seem as if White's attack is falling apart, as Black is
threatening White's minor pieces, and the kingside remains closed. If so,
the appearance is deceiving, and Jobava shows that Black's status is desperate. } (25... gxf4 26. Qg6+!! (26. Rdg1+ Kf8 27. Rg8+ $18 { is more than good enough, but Qg6+ is prettier and faster. }) 26... Kxg6 (26... Kf8 27. Qf7#) 27. Rdg1# { If the game ended like this it would be in combination books for the next
ten years. }) 26. Rh6! (26. Nxc4 dxc4 27. Qc2 $18) 26... Kxh6 (26... gxf4 27. Qg6+ Kf8 28. Qf7#) (26... Nxd6 27. Qg6+ Kf8 28. Bxd6+ Qxd6 29. Qxf6+ $18) 27. Nf7+ Kg7 28. Nxd8 Raxd8 29. b3 (29. Rg1) 29... Nb6 (29... Nd6 30. Bxd6 Rxd6 31. Qb5 $18) 30. Rg1 Rxh7 { Here White has two ways to win, and Jobava chooses the subtler method. } 31. e4! (31. Rxg5+? fxg5 32. Be5+ Kh6!) (31. Bxg5! fxg5 32. Rxg5+ Kh8 33. dxc5 Nd7 34. c6! Nac5 35. Qg6 bxc6 36. Rh5 Rxh5 37. Qxh5+ Kg7 38. Qg5+ Kf7 39. Qxd8 $18) 31... Kh8?! { It looks natural, and were it not for the problem that shows up at the
very end it would be Black's best try. } 32. e5!! gxf4 (32... Nd7 33. exf6) 33. Qg6 Rf8 34. exf6 { 1-0, Jobava,Baadur 2713 - Mamedyarov,Shakhriyar 2743, FIDE World Rapid
Ch, Dubai UAE (3.1), 2014.06.16 } *


