1. d4 Nf6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Bf4 { London bishop } { [%cal Rf4b8] } 3... c5 4. e3 cxd4 5. exd4 a6 { Prevents Nb5 } { [%cal Ra6b5,Bc3b5] } (5... Nc6 6. Nb5 e5 7. Bxe5 Nxe5 8. dxe5 Ng4 9. Qxd5 Qxd5 10. Nc7+ Kd7 11. Nxd5 Kc6 $16) 6. Bd3 Nc6 { Bd3 can be a target in future. } { [%cal Bc6b4,Rb4d3] } 7. Nge2 e6 { Solid. } 8. Qd2 { Castle on both sides is possible. } { [%cal Be1c1,Be1g1] } 8... b5 9. O-O { Black threatens pawn storm. Black Queen can quickly join attack on queen side. } { [%cal Yb5b4,Ya6a5,Yb4b3,Ya5a4,Bd8a5] } 9... Be7 10. a3 { Stops any Nb4 ideas. } { [%cal Ra3b4,Bc6b4] } 10... Bd7 11. h3 { Controls g5, make some room for a Bishop and King. } { [%cal Rh3g4,Bf4h2,Gg1h2] } 11... O-O 12. Rfe1 Na5 13. Rad1 { Centralising. } 13... Qb6 { Go to Puzzle V } { [%cal Rb6d4] } 14. Ng3 { [%cal Bg3f5,Ye1e7] } 14... Rfc8!? $140 { Loose bishop. } { [%csl Ye7] } (14... Qxd4 15. Bxh7+ Kxh7 16. Qxd4 $18) (14... Rfe8 $10) 15. Nf5 { Loose bishop. } { [%csl Ye7] } 15... exf5! (15... Bf8 16. Nh6+ gxh6 17. Bxh6 Bxh6 18. Qxh6 Ne8 { #4 }) 16. Rxe7 Be6 { With the idea of getting Re7 trapped. What should white do now? } { [%cal Ba5c6,Rc6e7] } 17. Bh6!! { Black has options take Bishop, attack rook, protect with knight or bring Queen back to defence. } { [%cal Rg7h6,Bg8f8,Rf8e7,Bf6h5,Gh5g7,Bb6d8,Rd8e7] } 17... gxh6?! (17... Kf8 18. Bxg7+ Kxe7 19. Bxf6+ Kd7 $16 { White pieces are well coordinated and King is very safe behind pawn wall on 1st rank. Black King is exposed on the center with weak, doubled pawns on f file. Be6 has no prospects. Na5 out of the game. } { [%csl Gf6,Gd3,Gc3,Gd2,Gd1,Gg1,Yd7,Ye6,Ya5,Yb6,Yc8,Ya8,Rf7,Rf5] } (19... Kxf6 { Puzzle VI } 20. Qh6+ Ke7 21. Nxd5+ Bxd5 22. Qxb6 $18)) (17... Qd8 { Even though Rook and Bishop are hanging white is great position now. } 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. Qg5+ Kh8 (19... Kf8 20. Qxf6 Qxe7 21. Qh8#) 20. Qxf6+ Kg8 21. Bxf5 Qxe7 (21... Bxf5 22. Qxf7+ Kh8 23. Qg7#) 22. Qxe7 Bxf5 $18 { [%csl Ge7] }) 18. Qxh6 Rxc3 19. Qg5+ Kf8 20. Qxf6 Rxd3 21. cxd3 { 1-0 Black resigns. } 21... Qd8 22. Re1 Nc6 23. R1xe6 Nxe7 24. Rxe7 Qxe7 (24... Qe8 25. Rxe8+ Rxe8) 25. Qh8# { 1-0, Baadur Aleksandrovich Jobava 2665 - Ruslan Ponomariov 2709, Chess
Olympiad, Baku AZE (8.2), 2016.09.10 } *


