[Black "Hikaru Nakamura 2767 - Anish Giri 2776"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[StudyName "The Jobava London System (Rapport-Jobava System) - Complete Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Example Games #1 - Hikaru Nakamura 2767 - Anish Giri 2776"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HaFTAegs/8ogq2VEO"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Shreksify"]

1. d4 { This was a game played by Hikaru Nakamura against Anish Giri. Both opposing sides played very well in the opening. Tremendous performance by both opposing sides until Anish made a huge blunder which eventually gave in the game. } 1... d5 2. Bf4 Nf6 3. e3 c5 { In this particular chapter we would be talking about the "Steinitz Countergambit"This effective Anti-London is known to quickly equalize or even have a better position against the London System. That is because after c5 all what white can do is react and defend consequently. After c5 Black would proceed with Qb6! White has to defend the b pawn or otherwise lose a piece.Despite of this, today's modern theory had successfully repealed and made amends to the Anti-London "Steinitz Countergambit" where white sacrifice a pawn for piece activity.But does this "Stenitz Countergambit" be an effective Anti-London against the Rapport-Jobava? We'll see... } 4. Nc3 cxd4 5. exd4 { Both opposing sides exchange pawns, black has two central pawns against white's one and only d central pawn. White's play would ultimately divert on the queenside. } 5... a6 { A fantastic move by Anish giri, prevents the Nb5 threat! } 6. Nf3 Bg4 7. Be2 e6 { Light square bishop out of the game! } 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Bxf3 { White gains a bishop pair but in exchange black gains the knight pair and also loosens white's grasp on the center. The knight on f3 is an effective support to pressurize the e5 square. } 9... Nc6 10. Ne2 Bd6 11. Be3 O-O 12. O-O Ne7 13. g3 { I know for a fact that Hikaru Nakamura often likes the find an outpost and reinforce his pieces in places which may sometimes seem unorthodox. } 13... Nf5 14. Bc1 Rc8 15. c3 b5 16. Qd3 Qc7 17. Bg5 Qc4 18. Rfd1 Be7 19. Nf4 { Exchanging queens would only benefit black since black's counterplay heavily relies on the queenside now. White would have to aim for the kingside but exchanging queens now would only extinguish part of white's attack. } 19... Nd7 20. Bxe7 Nxe7 21. Qd2 a5 22. Be2 Qc6 23. Bd3 Qb6 24. a4 bxa4 25. Rxa4 Rb8 26. Bc2 Qc7 27. Rda1 { The tables have turned, white is now attacking on the queenside. Successfully extinguished black's attack on the queenside. } 27... g6 28. Nd3 Nc6 29. R4a2 Nb6 30. h4 Rfe8 31. Qf4 Qxf4 32. Nxf4 { We are on the endgame now, white's attack would heavily rely on the queenside. Black's goal is to defend. White is on assault in here. } 32... Rec8 33. Bd1 Nc4 34. Nd3 Rb6 35. Ba4 Kg7 36. Kg2 Rc7 37. Re1 Nd8 38. Bd1 Nc6 39. Re2 h6 40. Nc5 Re7 41. Ba4 { Hikaru shuffles his pieces on the board hoping to find some chaos and counterplay which would ultimately result into his win. } 41... Ra7 42. Bb3 Nd6 43. Bc2 Re7 44. Bd3 Rb8 45. Re1 Rb6 46. Bc2 Ra7 47. Rea1 Rc7 48. Re1 Ra7 49. g4 Nb5 50. Ba4 Nd6 51. Rea1 Nd8 52. Bc2 Rc7 53. Ba4 N6b7 54. Nd7 Rd6 55. Ne5 Rb6 56. Re1 Nd6 57. Nd7 Rbb7 58. Nc5 Rb6 59. Bc2 N6b7 60. Na4 Rbc6 61. Ra3 Nd6 62. Bd3 N8b7 63. Rb3 Nc4 64. Bxc4 Rxc4 65. Ra1 R4c6 66. Rb5 Kf6 67. Kf3 { Warrior kings, trying to outflank one another. More of a waiting move I guess... } 67... Ke7 68. Ke2 Kf6 69. Kd3 g5 70. hxg5+ Kxg5 71. f3 f5 72. gxf5 Kxf5 73. Rh1 Rh7 74. b3 h5 75. c4 Kf4 76. cxd5 exd5 77. Rxd5 h4 78. Rh3? { +0.54/16 } (78. Nc3 { +2.08/15 } 78... Rb6 79. Ne2+ Kxf3 80. Rf5+ Kg4 81. Rf4+ Kg5 82. Rg1+ Kh5 83. Kd2 Rg6 84. Rxh4+ Kxh4 85. Rxg6 Rd7 86. Ke3) 78... Rg6?? { Interesting idea, black wants to exchange rooks with Rg3! But white's knight on a4 is dangerous. Nc3 Ne2! White is going to dislodge black's king from the kingside. } (78... Re6 { +0.54/16 } 79. Nc5 Rd6 80. Re5 Nxc5+ 81. Rxc5 Re6 82. Kc4 Re3 83. Rc8 Re1 84. Rf8+ Kg5 85. Kb5 Rc1 86. Kb6 Rd1 87. Rd8 Kf6) 79. Nc3 Rg3?! { +8.48/16 } (79... Re7 { +6.72/16 } 80. Ne2+ Rxe2 81. Kxe2 Rh6 82. Rd7 Nd6 83. Rg7 Kf5 84. Rg4 Re6+ 85. Kd3 Ne8 86. Re4 Rxe4 87. fxe4+ Kg4 88. Rh1 Nd6 89. e5 Nf5) 80. Ne2+ Kxf3 81. Nxg3 Kg4 82. Ne4 Kxh3 83. Ng5+ { 1-0, Hikaru Nakamura 2767 - Anish Giri 2776, London Chess Classic (Rapid Open), London ENG (8.1), 2014.12.07 } *


[Event "The Jobava London System (Rapport-Jobava System) - Complete Repertoire: Example Games #2 - Magnus Carlsen 2863 - Jeffery Xiong 2709"]
