1. d4 Nf6 { A45 Indian Defense } 2. Bf4 g6 { Nf6 black wants to divert the game into a King's Indian or a Semi-Grunfeld structure. But in here after the move Nc3! Black would have to play d4 thus transposing back to the position we studied with the Horwitz Defence. Well other than that if black refuses and play g6!? Then simply e4! The move pawn to e4 was made possible because of the knight on c3. Black would have to play d5 or d6!? We'll get into that. } 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 { There are two particular variations in which I recommend in here. The Austrian Attack or the "English" or "Yugoslavia" Attack structure. Everything heavily depends on your decision. } 4... d6 5. Qd2 O-O 6. O-O-O Nbd7 7. e5 Nh5 8. Bh6 c5?! { (0.52 → 1.28) Inaccuracy. Nb6 was best. } (8... Nb6 9. Bxg7 Nxg7 10. f4 { White expands... } 10... c6 11. Kb1 dxe5 12. fxe5 Nd5 13. Nf3 Bg4 14. Bc4 Nf5 15. Bxd5) 9. Bxg7 Nxg7 10. exd6 exd6 11. Nf3 Qa5 12. Kb1 Nf6 13. dxc5 { Queen takes a pawn is preferable in here. This would also support black's furious attack on the kingside. } 13... Be6?! { (1.27 → 1.87) Inaccuracy. Qxc5 was best. } (13... Qxc5 14. Qxd6 Qxd6 15. Rxd6 Be6 16. Be2 Nf5 17. Rdd1 Rad8 18. Rhe1 Rfe8 19. Bb5 Rxd1+ 20. Rxd1) 14. Nd5 Qd8 15. Nxf6+ Qxf6 16. cxd6 Nf5?? { (1.73 → 4.22) Blunder. Rad8 was best. } (16... Rad8 17. Qg5 Ne8 18. Qxf6 Nxf6 19. Rd4 Ne8 20. Be2 Rxd6 21. Rhd1 Rxd4 22. Nxd4 Ng7 23. c4) 17. Qf4 { The knight is pinned. Nowhere else to go after g4! } 17... h5 18. h3 h4 19. g4 hxg3 20. fxg3 Rac8 21. g4 Rc5 { The Attack was too much, losing the knight, free to resign. } 22. Bd3 { R Rapport 2700 - Getz N 2423, 2nd Vergani Cup 2018, https://lichess.org/study/AnndoYt3/k4tceEhw, } *


[Event "The Jobava London System (Rapport-Jobava System) - Complete Repertoire: Example Games #4 - Radjabov T 2724 - Anton Guijarro D 2631"]
