[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/HaFTAegs/uDbaaa1a"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Shreksify"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. Nc3 d5 { This particular chapter is dedicated to the"One" and "Only" main line Horwitz Defence against the Jobava London. The Horwitz Defence can basically be played against everything. By everything I mean "Everything". It can be played against the Queen's Gambit, Catalan Opening, English Opening, London System, King's Indian Attack, and so on.Basically in here black's simple plan is to occupy the center, reinforce a hasty attack either on the center or the queenside depending on white's play. So on and so forth in the Jobava, the system remains very different. The idea of Bf4 Nd5 is one of the key factor why the Horwitz's auto piloted move order wouldn't fit very well. Remember the idea of Bf4 Nd5! huh? Black either have to play a6 or Na6 just to stop this. Thus a tempo ahead and a complicatedly matter on the queenside makes this position quite unbearable! } 3. Bf4 { D01 Rapport-Jobava System D01 Rapport-Jobava System In this particular chapter we are going to talk about the one and only "Bf5" Symmetrical Jobava London."Black is following the same basic objective of the Rapport-Jobava. But in this scenario Black may have some ideas with putting off the light square bishop behind the pawn pyramid. e6-d5. But in here despite of black's logical idea Nb5! and Black would be forced to defend with Na6!? } { [%cal Re7e6,Rb7d5,Rc7c6,Rf7d5] } 3... c6 4. e3 Bf5 5. Nf3 e6 { This is the Slav Defence. The Slav Defence is known for it's dynamic and positional play. Despite it's reputation black's counterplay heavily relies on breakthroughs such as on the "Center" or "Queenside." In here the main line is usually c4 followed by a "Catalan" structure. But instead of c4 which is heavily theoretical. We would go for Nc3! Our Rapport-Jobava structure! } 6. Be2 h6 7. O-O Nbd7 8. Bd3 Bxd3 9. Qxd3 Bb4 10. a3 Bxc3 11. Qxc3 Ne4 12. Qd3 g5 13. Bg3 h5 14. Nd2 Nxd2 15. Qxd2 h4 16. Be5 f6 17. Bd6 Kf7 { Expands on the queenside... } 18. a4?! { (-0.01 → -0.67) Inaccuracy. h3 was best. } (18. h3 Nb6 19. Bh2 Nc4 20. Qe2 a5 21. b3 Nd6 22. c4 Ne4 23. Qb2 Qe7 24. c5 Rae8) 18... Nb6 19. Bb4 Nc4 20. Qc1 a5 21. Ba3 Nd6?! { (-0.87 → -0.29) Inaccuracy. h3 was best. } (21... h3) 22. Bxd6 Qxd6 23. Qd2 e5 24. Rad1 e4 25. f3 Qe6 26. f4 g4 { Black's attack on the kingside is dangerous! How can white make amends? } 27. b3 g3 28. h3 { White shuts down black's attack! } 28... Qf5 29. c4 Rhd8 30. Qe2 Kg6 31. Rc1 Qh5?? { (-0.05 → 1.87) Blunder. Rac8 was best. } (31... Rac8 32. Qd2) 32. f5+ Kh6 33. Qb2 Rd7 34. Rf4 Rg8?! { (1.95 → 2.73) Inaccuracy. Kg7 was best. } (34... Kg7 35. cxd5 cxd5 36. Qc3 Kf7 37. Qc2 Kg8 38. Re1 Qe8 39. Rxh4 Rg7 40. Rf1 Rc8 41. Qd2) 35. Rcf1 { Opens up the position for white. As black's pieces are wobby and badly organized. White would penetrate on the center. } 35... c5?? { (2.00 → 4.72) Blunder. Kg7 was best. } (35... Kg7 36. Qc3) 36. cxd5 Rxd5 37. dxc5 Rf8 38. Rxe4? { (5.26 → 3.33) Mistake. Qc2 was best. } (38. Qc2 Re8 39. c6 bxc6 40. Qxc6 Qf7 41. Rxh4+ Kg7 42. Rg4+ Kh7 43. Rxg3 Rg8 44. Rxg8 Kxg8) 38... Rd1 39. Rf4 Rxf1+ 40. Rxf1 { White has now successfuly extinguish black's counterplay on the center. White would now penetrate on the 7th rank. Although black's kingside safety is managable. Remember that the queenside pawns would all be sniped off. } 40... Kg7 41. Qd4!? { (4.17 → 1.71) Blunder. Qd2 was best. } (41. Qd2) 41... Rf7?? { (1.71 → 10.96) Blunder. Qe2 was best. } (41... Qe2 42. b4 axb4 43. Qxb4 Qxe3+ 44. Kh1 Rf7 45. Qxh4 Re7 46. Qg4+ Kf8 47. Qf3 Re4 48. Qxe3) 42. Rf4 Re7 43. Rg4+ Kf7?! { (15.13 → Mate in 3) Checkmate is now unavoidable. Qxg4 was best. } (43... Qxg4 44. Qxg4+) 44. Qd5+ { Radjabov T 2724 - Anton Guijarro D 2631, Fide World Blitz 2014, Dubai UAE, } *


