1. d4 { @Tangobob's "Jobava London System Game Collections" Come check it out! https://lichess.org/study/puCV36lu } 1... Nf6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Bf4 Bf5 4. f3 { "By not having developed the Knight to f3 [attacking d4 P], White can play the pawn [here on f3] and look towards a quick Kingside pawn advance which both gains space and pushes Black's pieces back." } 4... e6 5. g4 { "This natural follow up threatens to trap Black's light-squared Bishop. As you will know, when I can push 'Harry' (the h-pawn) I will, especially when it is the objectively best move." } 5... Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. e3 c5 { "This is the most active response [for black] but it is not without risk. As we will see in the theory section, 7...a6 is safer." } 8. h5 (8. Nb5 { GGM recommends this instead. }) 8... Bh7 9. Nb5 Na6 10. c3 Be7 11. Bd3 Bxd3 12. Qxd3 Nd7 13. Ne2 O-O 14. a4 Bf6 15. Bd6 Re8 16. f4 Nb6 17. g5 hxg5 18. h6 g6 19. fxg5 Bxg5 20. h7+ Kg7 21. Be5+ f6 22. Nd6 Rh8 23. Nf4 Bxf4 24. Bxf4 g5 25. O-O-O Nc4 26. Rh6 Kxh6 27. Nf7+ Kg7 28. Nxd8 Raxd8 29. b3 Nb6 30. Rg1 Rxh7 31. e4 Kh8 32. e5 gxf4 33. Qg6 Rf8 34. exf6 { 1-0, Baadur Aleksandrovich Jobava 2688 - Shakhriyar Mamedyarov 2799, World Rapid Championship, Dubai UAE (3.1), 2014.06.16 } *


